# Soulless Launcher — Module Ownership Audit & Refactor Roadmap

End-stage polish before updating the COSMIC PR. This document maps every
launcher module against the ownership rules below and lists what must change.
Work it top to bottom, one module/concern per commit, build green after each.

Context: first Rust project, ~2 months in. The architecture grew organically;
this refactor tightens ownership that growth revealed. Normal second phase —
build to learn what it needs, then refactor to match.

---

## THE TWO RULES

### Rule 1 — Module ownership
**Every module owns its own concern. No module reaches around another to load or
own what isn't its job. `main.rs` only builds and runs. `utils.rs` only does
string logic.**

### Rule 2 — Coordinator depth (app.rs)
**app.rs may only call the top-level `mod.rs` front door of each module. It must
never reach into a submodule's internals — no `registry::migrate::*`, no
`registry::load()`, no `search::indexer::*` from app.rs. If app.rs needs
something a module does internally, that module exposes a front-door method and
app.rs calls that. Logic lives behind the front door; app.rs only knocks.**

Corollary (logic depth): every arm of app.rs `update()` should *route*, not
*work*. An arm hands a message to the owning module's front door and returns. If
an arm is parsing, deciding, building commands, or spawning processes inline,
that work belongs behind a module front door. app.rs grows by adding more short
arms (safe, outward growth), never by deepening existing ones. A 1500-line
app.rs of short routes is healthy; a 300-line app.rs of deep tangled arms is not.

Dead-code warnings are *symptoms* of unfinished wiring, not junk to delete.
Where a warning marks scaffolding for a feature we are building (keyboard nav,
registry lookups, vault hidden-apps), we WIRE it. Anything genuinely abandoned
gets removed AND gets a tracking GitHub issue. Nothing stays `#[allow(dead_code)]`
without an issue behind it.

